<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Agriculture in the UK: A Key to Food Security and Sustainability</h1></b><br>
                Agriculture plays a vital role in the UK’s economy and food supply. However, like many countries, the UK
                faces challenges such as crop losses due to pests and diseases. Estimates suggest that 25–40% of six
                major crops grown worldwide are lost to pests annually (Scott et al., 2021), and while these are global
                figures, the UK is not immune to similar threats.<br><br>

                In 2020, 46% of the food consumed in the UK was imported, with fruit imports reaching 84% (Department
                for Environment, Food & Rural Affairs, 2023). This reliance on imports highlights the importance of
                strengthening domestic crop production and disease prevention strategies. Detecting plant diseases
                quickly and accurately can help enhance food security and protect agricultural economies.<br><br>

                Additionally, engaging students with interactive tools can foster interest in agriculture and raise
                awareness about food sustainability. Research supports the benefits of digital tools in
                education—Swinney (2016) highlights how schools in Scotland have already experienced the positive impact
                of digital technology, and Bond and Bedenlier (2019) emphasize that educational technology enhances
                student engagement when properly guided by instructors.<br><br>

                By integrating modern technology into agriculture education, students can gain valuable insights into
                crop health, sustainability, and the future of UK farming.
            </i></p>
